{"game": "space-marine-2011", "language": "english", "appid": 55150, "month": "2022-02", "window": 1, "start_date": 1643673600, "end_date": 1644278399, "month_true_total": 179, "quota": 5, "returned": 5, "pulled_at": "2026-10-09 18:01:15 UTC", "reviews": [{"recommendationid": "109751957", "author": {"steamid": "76561198247358758", "personaname": "TANDY", "persona_status": "offline", "profile_url": "https://steamcommunity.com/id/scriblerian/", "num_games_owned": 0, "num_reviews": 130, "playtime_forever": 1759, "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0, "playtime_at_review": 1759, "last_played": 1643330755, "avatar": "27dfefc0bbe768912b1a6d5a81f4203d41de1c29"}, "language": "english", "review": "Space Marine is a world devoid of action or personality. When not sporadically fighting hoards of generic goons, you will spend literal hours of gameplay wandering impossibly huge hallways devoid of any features. Never has a game more perfectly captured 40K's total lack of substance.\n\nStill, the game manages to play well. Players are mechanically incentivized to perform Mortal Kombat styled fatalities every few minutes, which gets repetitive but nails the grimderp tone of the setting. The final third of the campaign becomes a dazzling barrage of psychedelic particle effects that do a lot to make up for how drab its earlier sections appear. Tack on some suitably hammy voice acting, and the single-player experience is a lot of cheesy fun.", "timestamp_created": 1644278265, "timestamp_updated": 1644278265, "voted_up": true, "votes_up": 2, "votes_funny": 0, "weighted_vote_score": "0.499631255865097046", "comment_count": 0, "steam_purchase": false, "received_for_free": false, "refunded": false, "written_during_early_access": false, "primarily_steam_deck": false, "app_release_date": "1315281600", "reactions": []}, {"recommendationid": "109748489", "author": {"steamid": "76561198343267604", "personaname": "Adelram", "persona_status": "offline", "profile_url": "https://steamcommunity.com/profiles